WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.660
Choosing someone to take over a role from
another actor is never easy, especially when

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.339
the original actor drew praise for their take
on a character.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.420
But recasting is often an unavoidable fact
of filmmaking, especially for sequels and

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:11.920
remakes.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.140
Whether it's creative disagreements, financial
setbacks, or simple timing issues at hand,

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.689
actors often become unavailable to resume
their roles and must be replaced.

00:00:20.689 --> 00:00:24.529
Sometimes, the newcomer can blend right into
the blockbuster scene unnoticed.

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:29.460
But in the case of these casting switcheroos,
the change was just too drastic for audiences

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:29.960
to endure.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.020
Clarice Starling

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:37.670
When Ridley Scott signed on to direct the
Silence of the Lambs sequel, he did so without

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.400
his Clarice Starling in place.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.940
Anthony Hopkins had already negotiated a return
to the role of Hannibal Lecter, but Jodie

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.859
Foster didn't share his enthusiasm for the
second chapter in the serial killer's story,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.140
despite winning an Oscar for the role in the
first film.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.659
She later told Total Film, "The official reason
I didn't do Hannibal is I was doing another

00:00:56.659 --> 00:00:57.659
movie, Flora Plum.

00:00:57.659 --> 00:01:02.280
So I get to say, in a nice, dignified way,
that I wasn't available when that movie was

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:02.820
being shot.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.420
But Clarice meant so much to [Lambs director
Jonathan Demme] and I … there was no way

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.420
that either of us could really trample on
her."

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.900
"Now that's really interesting, Clarice.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.320
I'm really pressed for time."

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.700
Several big name actresses were considered
to take her place, and the part ultimately

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.780
went to Julianne Moore.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.640
While both Scott and Hopkins were happy with
the choice, audiences weren't as convinced.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.079
Moore was inevitably compared to Foster and
couldn't measure up to her predecessor's screen

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.900
savvy nor rekindle the creepy connection Clarice
had shared with Hopkins in the first film.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.780
Jodie Foster offered her own diplomatic but
shady take on the film, saying, simply, "I

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.280
saw Hannibal.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.140
I won't comment."

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.360
Evelyn O'Connell

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.540
The Mummy was a surprise smash for Universal,
posting global receipts of $415 million and

00:01:50.549 --> 00:01:54.170
landing on the top 10 highest-grossing films
of 1999.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:58.219
It launched Brendan Fraser's career as a Hollywood
action hero and introduced the world to a

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.680
stunning new leading lady in Rachel Weisz.

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.200
The pair made a compelling onscreen couple
and reprised their roles in The Mummy Returns,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.320
which managed to bring in a nice haul from
the box office as well.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:13.040
But by the time the third film came around,
Weisz was suffering from some serious Mummy

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.580
fatigue and stepped aside.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.480
Weisz was rumored to call the script for the
Mummy 3 "crap," so the studio turned to Maria

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:20.790
Bello to fill in for the role.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.200
"That should give us a good advantage."

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.100
Unfortunately for Bello, her turn in the role
was criticized for lacking the charm of the

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.260
original and the film failed to recoup its
production costs in the domestic box office.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.980
"Hahhahaa, why am I laughing?"

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:36.960
The Genie

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.950
The '90s marked a moment of resurgence for
Disney's animated film collection, with films

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.760
like Beauty and the Beast and The Lion King
offering a new generation of children some

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.420
cartoon movies to sing along to.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.100
One of Disney's most beloved films of the
era was Aladdin, an Oscar-winning take on

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.700
the Arabic folk tale about the street thief
who shed his rags and won the heart of a princess.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.560
Aladdin was packed full of great musical numbers,
but the main reason it became such a smash

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.190
was the input of the late Robin Williams as
the Genie.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.900
"It's all part and parcel of the Genie gig.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.360
Phenomenal cosmic power …in an itty bitty living
space."

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:16.040
The actor confirmed that he wouldn't be lending
his voice to the direct-to-video sequel The

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.760
Return of Jafar after an alleged row with
Disney over its marketing tactics, so the

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.160
search for a new Genie began.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.360
Dan Castellaneta, the legendary voice of Homer
Simpson, was ultimately chosen to voice the

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.340
big blue guy, but the Simpsons star just couldn't
measure up to Williams' frenetic performance.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.650
The quality of the animation didn't help matters,
but Castellaneta lacked the unpredictability

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:42.340
that Williams brought to the Genie, essentially
doing a watered-down impression of his predecessor.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:43.480
"D'oh!"

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:45.320
Jack Ryan

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:51.220
There are five films based on Tom Clancy's
Jack Ryan books, but only one actor has played

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.300
the protagonist twice.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.970
Eyebrows were raised when Alec Baldwin was
given the boot after he successfully debuted

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.900
Ryan on-screen in The Hunt for the Red October.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:03.470
Baldwin later implied that the studio had
gone behind his back and entered into negotiations

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.600
with Ford to take the role of Ryan.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.290
"Well that was a guess, but it seemed logical."

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:13.460
Even so, Ford's performance in Patriot Games
and Clear and Present Danger became definitive

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.040
for the character.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.440
Eight years later, another actor stepped in
to bring a much younger version of Jack Ryan

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:19.160
to the big screen.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:23.060
This time, it was Ben Affleck who stepped
in for The Sum of All Fears.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.780
His performance in the role was met with disdain,
however, and the Jack Ryan franchise never

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.260
really recovered after that, despite an attempt
to reboot it with Chris Pine in the lead more

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.280
than a decade later.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:34.820
Sarah Connor

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.590
Following in the footsteps of Linda Hamilton
as Sarah Connor in the Terminator franchise

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.680
was never going to be easy for any actress.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.139
Hamilton gave a performance so layered and
compelling that even the most talented performers

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.880
would have struggled to do the character justice.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.980
The first to give it a shot was Lena Headey,
who was cast in the role in the short-lived

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.880
TV series Terminator: The Sarah Connor Chronicles.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:00.580
Fox cancelled the show after two seasons citing
its high cost as a reason, but Headey's Game

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.900
of Thrones colleague Emilia Clarke was willing
to give it another go on the silver screen

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.340
for Terminator: Genisys.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.020
The film was a box office dud and was heavily
criticized for using the old time travel chestnut

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.520
to rewrite the beginning of the Terminator
story to suit the filmmakers' purposes.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.159
The main problem wasn't the infuriatingly
muddled timeline, though.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.200
It was the casting.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:27.139
As much as TV lovers might dig Clarke's portrayal
of Daenerys Targaryen in Game of Thrones,

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:32.720
the film crowd was decidedly not wowed by
her run as Sarah Connor, where her line deliveries

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.760
packed a lot less punch than the original.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.460
"Come with me if you want to live."

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:37.320
Pass.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:39.340
Van Wilder

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.360
National Lampoon's Van Wilder isn't going
to be preserved in the Library of Congress

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.870
National Film Registry anytime soon.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:50.440
Yet for all its failings, fans of a certain
age remember this movie fondly.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:55.759
This was a time when frat humor was at its
peak, and sitting down to watch a comedy usually

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.880
meant turning your brain off for 90 minutes.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:02.979
Van Wilder might not have been the most cerebral
comedy of all time, but Ryan Reynolds still

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:07.889
delivered as the fast-talking cool guy who
threw the best parties and was a softie underneath

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:09.080
all those crazy antics.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:14.260
After the Kal Penn-led sequel was destroyed
by critics, the producers decided their next

00:06:14.279 --> 00:06:17.460
move would be a prequel featuring a younger
version of Van Wilder.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.379
Reynolds refused to return to the role, so
they brought in Mean Girls' Jonathan Bennett

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.949
for Van Wilder: Freshman Year — which was
a terrible mistake.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.020
"I have never seen such a sorry display in
all my life."

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.020
The actor couldn't come close to the charisma
of his predecessor and produced a feature-length

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.760
failed impression effort as a result.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:36.680
Willie Mays Hayes

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.280
Major League was a surprise grand slam for
Paramount Pictures and produced two increasingly

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:43.140
terrible sequels.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:47.520
And while Charlie Sheen's Wild Thing came
back for a second round at the mound in Major

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.569
League II, there was another standout actor
from the first film who refused to run the

00:06:51.569 --> 00:06:53.180
bases of this franchise again.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:58.419
Wesley Snipes, whose charming swagger as the
speedy base thief was a homerun in the first

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:01.690
movie, had no interest in returning to the
dugout for the second movie.

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:06.379
At the time, he was well on his way to becoming
a Hollywood MVP in his own right, thanks to

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:10.590
movies like White Men Can't Jump and Demolition
Man, so he pretty much priced himself out

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:11.340
of the second movie.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.340
With the Cleveland Indians down a man on the
roster, the filmmakers turned to relative

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:19.240
newcomer Omar Epps to sub in as pinch runner
for the project.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:24.259
Unfortunately, Epps didn't have a strong background
in comedy and his performance was the cinematic

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:25.440
equivalent of a foul ball.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:27.360
"Read some of your reviews man.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.700
One guy said Willie Mays Hayes couldn't play
dead."

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.120
Just a bit outside...

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.280
Albus Dumbledore

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:39.039
The Prisoner of Azkaban might still be a favorite
among some Harry Potter fans, thanks to the

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:42.780
arrival of Sirius Black and that wild whomping
willow.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.099
But the fact remains that it's the lowest
grossing movie in the franchise, and for many,

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:52.020
the subdued enthusiasm seems to stem from
the unfortunate arrival of a new Hogwarts

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.200
headmaster in the film.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.919
Richard Harris' take on Albus Dumbledore in
the first two movies had become definitive

00:07:57.920 --> 00:07:58.720
for Potter fans.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:03.011
So when the actor died after a battle with
Hodgkin's disease, the filmmakers had to turn

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.240
to another actor to step into the beloved
professor's cloak.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:10.879
Michael Gambon seemed like a fine choice to
conjure up for the franchise, but his performance

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:15.340
in his first run as Dumbledore was stunted
by the fact that he never read the books.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:20.330
Rather than resuming Harris' spellbindingly
calm and wise demeanor, Gambon delivered a

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:24.520
much more stern and aggressive version of
the character for his first few outings.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:25.540
"Harry!

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.480
Did you put your name in that Goblet of Fire?"

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:27.980
"No sir!"

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.280
"Did you ask one of the older students to
do it?

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:30.780
"No sir."

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:32.820
"Are you absolutely sure?

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:33.440
"Yes sir."

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:37.840
Gambon would later summon up some sort of
chill pill that saved the role from being

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.260
ruined for children across the globe, but
these moments of mismatching between the beloved

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:46.450
book character and the screen interpretation
still cost the movies many house points in

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.380
the eyes of the true Potter fanatics.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:50.560
Catwoman

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:55.500
Halle Berry taking over Michelle Pfeiffer's
role in Catwoman is perhaps the most infamous

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.250
example of recasting going terribly wrong.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:02.660
DC's leather-clad heroine has appeared in
numerous Batman adaptations over the years,

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:07.680
but Pfeiffer's psychotically sexy performance
in Tim Burton's Batman Returns remains the

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:08.260
cat's meow.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:14.680
"I don't know about you Miss Kitty but I feel
so much yummier."

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:18.660
The actress initially intended to reprise
the role in the solo spinoff.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:23.680
But after years of development woes, her patience
reached its limit and she decided to concentrate

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:24.480
on other commitments.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:29.740
After a rigorous recruiting process, Berry
was ultimately chosen to climb into the catsuit

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.830
— and what a mess it all was.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:36.150
The film was a complete disaster, bombing
hard at the box office while cleaning up at

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:36.650
the Razzies.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:41.640
Berry took her Worst Actress win in good humor
and even turned up to accept her award in

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:42.140
person.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:43.800
Batman

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.350
Another error of judgment from the Batman
universe occurred when Michael Keaton handed

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.320
over the keys for Wayne Manor to Val Kilmer.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:56.270
Keaton had portrayed the role in 1989's Batman
and its sequel Batman Returns, but he refused

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:59.460
to reprise the role in a third film after
reading the screenplay.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.940
When pressed for details, Keaton later said
that the script he was shown "sucked" and

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:07.840
that "it was just awful," pointing the blame
for his departure squarely at the writers.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.590
Val Kilmer clearly didn't share Keaton's opinion.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:14.690
The Tombstone star accepted the role and went
into the film knowing he'd have to contend

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:18.430
with a wide cast of wacky villains as well
as a new director.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:22.750
Joel Schumacher ultimately shouldered a lot
of the blame for the badness of Batman Forever,

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.110
though his leading man didn't escape criticism.

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.260
"What am I?"

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:32.280
"Please, you're as blind as a bat."

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:35.840
He was quickly replaced with George Clooney
in Batman &amp; Robin.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:40.240
The good news for Kilmer was that Clooney's
turn as the The Dark Knight wasn't any more

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.520
loved than his own, and soon everyone forgot
about Kilmer's turn in the batmobile.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.120
"Forget it, take it off"

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:49.340
"It's Val Kilmer!"

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:51.900
"Oh.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:52.820
You're an actor?"

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.000
Thanks for watching!

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.140
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.520
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

